-- title:
--   Integral closure of K[x] spanned by x^jL(M₁D)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and let $x \in F$ be transcendental over $K$ with $F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$. Here a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ that contains the image of $K$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring; $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ denotes $-\log$ of the associated adic valuation of $f$ at $v$, a divisor is a finitely supported integer-valued function on places, and $\mathcal L(D) =$ `LSpace D` is the $K$-subspace of those $f \in F$ whose adic valuation at each $v$ is at most $\exp(D\,v)$. Let $D$ be a divisor with $D\,v = \max(0, -\operatorname{ord}_v x)$ at every place $v$, i.e. the pole divisor of $x$. The assertion is that there exists a natural number $M_1$ such that every $h \in F$ satisfying $\operatorname{ord}_v h \ge 0$ at every place $v$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v x \ge 0$ lies in the $K$-linear span of the set of elements of the form $x^j y$ with $j \in \mathbb N$ and $y \in \mathcal L(M_1 \cdot D)$.
--
--   The elements $h$ described in the hypothesis are exactly those integral over $K[x]$, namely the elements of the intersection of the valuation rings of the places not above $x = \infty$; the conclusion says that this ring is generated as a $K[x]$-module by the single Riemann–Roch space $\mathcal L(M_1 D)$ attached to one multiple of the pole divisor of $x$. It is used in the construction of regular prolongations, in the bounds of [`AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_forall_finrank_residueSpan_inf_add_card_le`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.RegularProlongation.exists_forall_finrank_residueSpan_inf_add_card_le) and its algebraically closed variant.
